  [1mThe fork.[0m Adding a [38;5;153muserId[39m [3mparameter[0m means some caller has to supply it — but the only caller is a pre-authentication
  form submit, and at that moment nothing in the app knows a user ID. That's the gap I can't resolve from the code.
  Three readings, and they differ in what the interface looks like afterward:

  - [1m[38;5;153muserId[39m is a return value, not a parameter[0m — the server knows the ID once credentials check out, so [38;5;153mlogin()[39m returns
    it in the result object and the caller logs it. Matches the stub's existing shape ([38;5;153m{ success, user }[39m); cheap to
    change later. Costs nothing now, but it's not literally what you asked for.
  - [1m[38;5;153muserId[39m is a real parameter[0m — signature becomes [38;5;153mlogin(userId, username, password)[39m or an options object. Honest to the
    request, but the caller has no value to pass, so it'd be [38;5;153mnull[39m/[38;5;153mundefined[39m at the only call site until something
    upstream produces one. Locks in a signature that other callers would have to satisfy.
  - [1m[38;5;153musername[39m already is the identifier[0m — skip the parameter and just make the existing [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m a proper tracking
    call keyed on username. Smallest change; rules out per-user IDs distinct from usernames later.

  [1mMy recommendation: the first.[0m "Track who logged in" is information the login [3mproduces[0m, not information it needs, and
  the stub already returns a user field — extending the return value is the change that's still correct once this talks
  to a real API. If you have an upstream source of user IDs I can't see, tell me and the second option becomes right.

  One more thing: if "track" means wiring up actual analytics or an audit log — a new tracking module, persistence, an
  event schema — that's a new subsystem and I'd re-classify this as architectural and write a spec instead. I'm assuming
  for now you mean local logging in this stub.

